// Original ZUNO fiction. Each band is a separately written edition.
const story=(id,title,skill,editions,reflect,related,imageKey)=>({id,title,question:'Can I read '+title+'?',category:'stories',kind:'story',minutes:4,tag:'Original story · '+skill,words:'story stories fiction '+title.toLowerCase()+' '+skill.toLowerCase(),fact:skill,source:'ZUNO original fiction · fictional characters',url:null,imageKey,levels:Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(editions).map(([k,v])=>[k,v[0]])),sectionsByAge:Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(editions).map(([k,v])=>[k,v.slice(1)])),sections:editions['9-11'].slice(1),reflect,related});
export const extraStories=[
story('bridge-for-beetle','A Bridge for a Beetle','Design for someone else',{
'6-8':['Imani found a toy beetle beside a puddle in the school garden. She wanted to make it a bridge. Her first bridge was a long strip of paper. It looked lovely until the middle dipped into the water.', ['Try a fold','Her friend Jo folded another strip like a little fan. They rested it between two dry stones. The toy crossed without getting wet. Imani drew the folds in her notebook.'],['A different traveller','Then Jo brought a wider toy. It did not fit. They made the bridge wider and tried again, very gently. A good bridge had to suit its traveller.'],['Leave the garden well','They took their paper and toys indoors. A real beetle walked past on a leaf. Imani watched without moving it. “You already have a road,” she whispered.']],
'9-11':['Imani wanted the garden display to include a bridge. She chose a paper strip, balanced it over a shallow tray and sent a toy beetle across. The middle sagged. “It needs to be stronger,” she said. Jo asked how they would know whether the next design was better.', ['A useful comparison','They used two strips cut from the same sheet. One stayed flat. They folded the other back and forth. With the supports the same distance apart, they placed the same toy on each. The folded strip held the toy more steadily. Their teacher reminded them that this result described their paper models, not every bridge in the world.'],['Who is it for?','A wider toy got stuck between the folded edges. Imani had solved one problem and discovered another. They widened the crossing, added low sides and checked it with several toys. Jo drew a tiny ramp at either end. They tested that change too, rather than assuming the drawing would work.'],['A better display','Their final label showed the first failure beside the finished model. Visitors could try the toys and explain which features helped. Imani’s favourite comment came from a younger child: “You thought about who would cross.” The children cleared every scrap from the garden before going home.']],
'12-14':['Imani’s first bridge was elegant in the sketchbook and disappointing over the demonstration tray. A single paper strip sagged under a toy beetle. She proposed adding more paper. Jo suggested defining the problem first: a model crossing for several toy users, using one sheet and two supports, without touching the water below.', ['Evidence with boundaries','They compared a flat strip with a folded one, keeping the paper, support spacing and toy constant. The folded version performed better in that setup. They recorded the result without turning it into a universal claim about engineering. The folds changed the shape; the test did not tell them every reason for the difference or how a full-sized structure would behave. A teacher helped them plan safe tabletop checks.'],['Success for whom?','Their narrow design worked for the beetle and failed for a broad wheeled toy. A steep entry ramp caused another difficulty. “Strong enough” was only one requirement. They made a list: width, stable entry, usable sides, limited material and easy repair. They asked younger classmates to try the models, explaining that feedback was about the design rather than the designer. One child noticed an awkward corner neither inventor had considered.'],['A record worth keeping','The finished display included photographs of the models, a comparison table and a clear note about the limits of the experiment. Jo wanted to hide the bent first attempt. Imani put it at the front. It showed why the requirements had changed. Their design had improved because they watched users, controlled a few variables and admitted what remained uncertain. They collected all the scraps afterwards. The real garden wildlife needed neither their model nor their litter.']]},
{'6-8':'Why did the wider toy need a different bridge?','9-11':'Which things stayed the same in the comparison?','12-14':'How did feedback change the design requirements?'},['patterns','data','sensors'],'robot-story'),
story('quiet-bench','The Quiet Bench Club','Listen before you solve',{
'6-8':['At break time, Zuri liked a quiet bench near the classroom. One day it was covered in bags. “Nobody cares about quiet places,” she said. Her friend Eli asked the others why the bags were there.', ['Ask first','The children said their hooks were full. They had put the bags on the bench to keep them off the wet ground. They had not known Zuri wanted to sit there.'],['Make room','With their teacher, the class chose a dry place for the bags. They left half the bench clear and drew a small sign with a book on it.'],['A shared place','The next day, Eli sat beside Zuri and drew quietly. Other children joined them. It was not a secret club. Anyone could use the quiet space and help look after it.']],
'9-11':['Zuri called it the Quiet Bench Club, although the club had one member and no rules. She liked sketching there after a noisy lesson. On Monday, the bench vanished beneath a mountain of schoolbags. Zuri decided the others had taken it over on purpose. Eli suggested asking before writing an angry sign.', ['The missing information','The bag hooks beside the classroom were full. Rain had left the ground damp. Several children thought the bench was the most sensible dry place to leave their things. Nobody had connected the pile of bags with Zuri’s missing quiet corner. Knowing that did not solve the problem, but it changed the conversation.'],['Two needs, one plan','Their teacher helped them list what people needed: dry storage, a place to sit and a clear walkway. They tried a different storage arrangement and kept part of the bench free. A drawing of a book explained that quiet activities were welcome. Zuri checked that the sign did not suggest some children were forbidden to sit there.'],['A club that changed','A week later, the class reviewed the arrangement. It worked on most days, but a visiting class needed extra storage. They adjusted the plan. Zuri still liked the name Quiet Bench Club. Now it meant listening to several needs, rather than defending a piece of furniture as if it belonged only to her.']],
'12-14':['When the classroom bench disappeared under schoolbags, Zuri read the scene as a message: her classmates did not respect her quiet space. She began drafting a sharp notice. Eli asked a question that annoyed her at first. What evidence showed that the bags had been placed there to exclude her?', ['Separate observation from motive','The bench was covered. That was observable. The intention behind the bags was a guess. The hooks were full, the ground was wet and the children had chosen a dry surface. They had not discussed how the bench was used. Zuri’s frustration was real even though her explanation was incomplete. Once she separated those two things, she could describe the problem without accusing everyone of an intention she could not establish.'],['Agree on the problem','With their teacher, the class identified competing needs: accessible storage, a clear route, somewhere to sit and somewhere relatively quiet. They tested a new bag arrangement and reserved part of the bench for quiet activities. Their sign described the activity rather than deciding which people belonged. Zuri also asked children who did not usually speak in class whether the plan created difficulties for them. A younger pupil pointed out that the bags could still block the approach.'],['A rule can be revised','After a week, the class reviewed what had happened. They needed a temporary arrangement when visitors arrived, and the sign needed a clearer picture. The plan was not a permanent victory for one group. It was a shared response to a specific problem. Zuri kept her original angry draft in her notebook. Beside it she wrote three headings: what I saw, what I assumed, and what I learned by asking. The new headings were useful in far more places than the Quiet Bench Club.']]},
{'6-8':'Why were the bags on the bench?','9-11':'How did asking change Zuri’s understanding?','12-14':'Which sentence could describe the problem without assuming another person’s motive?'},['data','online-safety','patterns'],'library-story'),
story('seed-envelope','The Envelope with No Name','Keep records that help',{
'6-8':['Kito opened an envelope in the garden cupboard. It held seeds, but the label was blank. “They must be beans,” he said. Asha thought they were something else. Their teacher said guessing would not give the seeds a name.', ['A careful start','They did not taste the seeds. Their teacher chose a safe place to grow them. Kito drew their shape and wrote the date. Asha marked the pot with the same little triangle as the envelope.'],['Keep the clues together','Each time they looked at the pot, they added a drawing. They kept the envelope beside their notes. Nothing got mixed with the other plants.'],['A useful habit','Later, their teacher helped them identify the growing plant. Kito made a new label. It had a name, a date and a picture. Next time, the envelope would tell a better story.']],
'9-11':['Kito found an unlabelled seed envelope in the school garden cupboard. He wanted to announce that it contained beans. Asha was not convinced. Their teacher explained that identifying an unknown plant from a quick guess was unreliable, and nobody should taste the seeds. The class could investigate with adult supervision and careful records.', ['Give the mystery a label','They assigned the envelope a simple code and copied it onto a pot. Kito sketched the seeds. Asha recorded when and where their teacher planted them. They kept the original envelope. A name could be added later; a reliable link between the seeds, pot and observations was needed now.'],['The notebook problem','A week later, Kito wrote “much bigger”. Asha asked bigger than what. They began noting the date of each observation and drawing beside a ruler placed by the teacher. When they missed a day, they left a gap instead of inventing a measurement. Another group could now understand what the notes meant.'],['A name with a history','Once the plant had grown enough, their teacher helped the class identify it using an appropriate reference. Kito added the name without erasing the old code. The record showed how the conclusion had developed. Their cupboard gained a new rule: a useful label belongs on the packet before anyone forgets what is inside.']],
'12-14':['The envelope was ordinary: brown paper, a few seeds and a label rubbed blank. Kito recognised the shape and wanted to name the contents immediately. Asha wanted to preserve the uncertainty. Their teacher established a practical boundary first: unknown seeds were not food, and any growing investigation would be supervised.', ['Identity before identification','The class gave the envelope a temporary code, marked the planting pot with the same code and recorded who had planted it, when and under which conditions. They retained the packet. The code did not identify the species, but it prevented observations from becoming detached from the material being studied. Asha called it a name for the question while they worked towards a name for the plant.'],['Make the record interpretable','Kito’s first note said “much bigger today”. The phrase captured excitement rather than a useful comparison. They agreed on a consistent way to describe growth and included dates, units where relevant and clear drawings. They did not fill a missed observation with an estimated value disguised as a measurement. When a pot was moved, they noted the change. A neat table could still mislead if it hid changes in the setup.'],['Conclusions have a route','Their teacher eventually helped them use the developing plant and a suitable reference to make an identification. They kept the temporary code beside the new name, allowing someone else to follow the record backwards. The class could distinguish observations from the later conclusion and see which evidence supported it. Kito was proud of the correct label. Asha was proud that another group could understand how it had been reached. In the cupboard, they found several more packets whose labels needed attention. This time they repaired the records before starting another mystery.']]},
{'6-8':'What should you do instead of tasting an unknown seed?','9-11':'Why was “much bigger” a difficult note to use?','12-14':'Why keep the temporary code after identifying the plant?'},['data','sensors','water-cycle'],'rain-story'),
story('shadow-clock','The Clock with No Batteries','Notice a changing pattern',{
'6-8':['Lulu drew around the shadow of a post with chalk. After lunch, she came back. The post was in the same place, but the shadow was not. “Someone moved my drawing!” she said. Her brother smiled and pointed at the empty chalk outline.', ['Look again','They watched from a safe shady spot. Their teacher helped them mark a new outline and write down the time. They did not look straight at the Sun.'],['Two drawings','The new shadow had a different place and shape. Lulu drew both in her book. She gave each drawing its own time.'],['A question for tomorrow','Would the shadow change in the same way tomorrow? Lulu wanted to find out. She left her chalk with the teacher and kept her notebook ready. The post had become a question she could visit again.']],
'9-11':['Lulu traced a post’s shadow before the morning bell. When she returned after lunch, the shadow no longer fitted the outline. Her first theory involved a mischievous classmate and a very large eraser. Her teacher suggested investigating the shadow rather than blaming an invisible prankster.', ['Choose what to record','They used the same post and marked where each outline began. Alongside every drawing, Lulu recorded the time. She watched the ground, never the Sun directly. Her brother noted when clouds made the edge difficult to see. That observation mattered too: an unclear outline was not the same as no shadow at all.'],['Find a pattern carefully','The shadows differed in direction and length during the school day. Lulu made a page showing their order. She could describe the pattern without pretending that the post itself had walked. Their teacher connected the observations with the Sun’s changing apparent position in the sky. The schoolyard offered a useful model, though clouds and the uneven surface complicated the drawings.'],['One day is a beginning','Lulu wanted to turn the post into a perfect clock. Her teacher asked what they would need to check on other days. Lulu added a new page called “Questions still open”. The project became more interesting when the notebook had room for uncertainty as well as colourful lines.']],
'12-14':['A row of chalk marks around the schoolyard post looked like a puzzle nobody had finished. Lulu had traced its morning shadow, then returned to find that the dark shape had shifted. She wanted to build a clock without batteries. Her teacher asked her to begin with observations before promising a reliable timekeeper.', ['A consistent observation point','The group chose one fixed post, marked a reference point and recorded the time of each outline. They watched the ground rather than looking directly at the Sun. They noted clouds, surface irregularities and occasions when the shadow boundary was indistinct. Those conditions affected how confidently they could compare the marks. They also kept measurements separate from sketches made later from memory.'],['A pattern needs an explanation','Across the day, the shadow’s length and direction changed. Their teacher helped them connect that pattern with the Sun’s apparent motion as Earth rotates. Lulu’s diagram showed the sequence more clearly than a single dramatic photograph would have. Yet the observations did not justify calling the chalk marks a dependable clock for every date and location. The group listed assumptions that had been hidden in the first ambitious claim.'],['Improve the question','Instead of asking whether their shadow clock was finished, they asked which observations would help evaluate it. They planned comparisons on other suitable days and a discussion of seasonal change using trusted learning material. They would need to consider how the reference system related to ordinary clock time. Lulu redesigned the display to include a changing-pattern investigation alongside the model clock. Visitors could try to explain what the marks showed and what they did not show. The unfinished part became an invitation to think, rather than an embarrassment to cover with brighter chalk.']]},
{'6-8':'What changed while the post stayed still?','9-11':'Why did the group write the time beside each outline?','12-14':'What extra checks would a useful shadow timekeeper need?'},['sun','solar-system','patterns'],'rain-story'),
story('mended-kite','The Kite with Two Tails','Change one thing at a time',{
'6-8':['Mina’s paper kite spun in the wind and landed beside her shoes. She wanted to change its string, its tail and its shape all at once. Grandpa asked which change she would learn from if it flew better.', ['One small try','They chose a safe open place, far from roads and wires. Grandpa helped check the kite. Mina changed only its tail. They tried it gently.'],['Watch, then write','The kite behaved differently. Mina drew a picture and told Grandpa what she had seen. She did not call one short flight a perfect test.'],['A flying notebook','They tried again another day. Mina’s notebook filled with tails, loops and arrows. Her favourite kite was patched in several colours. Every patch reminded her of a question she had taken time to explore.']],
'9-11':['Mina’s kite turned sharply and settled on the grass. She had a list of fixes ready: new string, longer tail, different shape and perhaps a heroic amount of tape. Grandpa asked how she would discover which change helped if she made all four at once. Mina put down the tape.', ['Set a safe boundary','They checked the kite together and chose an open area away from roads, power lines and other people. Grandpa supervised every flight. They agreed to try one small change to the tail while leaving the rest of the model alone. Mina drew the original version before altering it.'],['The wind has a vote','One trial looked more stable than the first flight. Mina nearly wrote “problem solved”. Then the wind dropped, and the next attempt told a different story. She began recording the conditions she could notice, not just whether she liked the result. Keeping a design change simple did not make the outdoor conditions identical.'],['Keep the useful mistake','Over several visits, Mina learned to describe what happened without declaring every good flight a final answer. Her patched kite looked less tidy than a shop display, but her notebook was much more useful than a list of confident guesses. When a younger cousin asked for help, she began with a question: “Which one thing shall we try first?”']],
'12-14':['Mina’s first response to a failed kite flight was to redesign everything. A longer tail, a new attachment point, extra tape and a different string might all help, she argued. Grandpa pointed out the difficulty: even if the next flight improved, several simultaneous changes would leave the explanation unclear.', ['Control what you can','They inspected the model together and chose a safe open space well away from roads, overhead wires and bystanders. Grandpa supervised the activity. Mina documented the original design and made one modest alteration. She kept the material and arrangement as consistent as she could, while recognising that an outdoor test would never offer perfectly controlled conditions. Her notebook included predictions written before each attempt.'],['An encouraging result is not a conclusion','The first altered flight looked steadier. The second coincided with a noticeable change in wind. Mina had to decide whether to discard the awkward result. Instead, she recorded it and described why the comparison was uncertain. She could report that the kite had flown differently without claiming that the tail modification alone had caused the difference. Grandpa reminded her that a careful account of limitations was useful information, not a confession of failure.'],['A design with a memory','Across later supervised trials, Mina refined both the kite and her way of investigating it. She learned to label each version so an observation could be linked to the correct design. The final kite carried several visible patches. She left them in place for the school exhibition, beside the first sketch and a page of unanswered questions. A cousin wanted her to supply the perfect design immediately. Mina offered something more portable: a way to make a prediction, change a limited feature, observe carefully and decide what the evidence could reasonably support.']]},
{'6-8':'Why did Mina try one small change?','9-11':'What made the two outdoor trials hard to compare?','12-14':'How could recording predictions help Mina interpret later results?'},['data','patterns','sensors'],'robot-story'),
story('map-of-sounds','The Map You Could Hear','Describe without collecting private details',{
'6-8':['Tari wanted to draw a map of the playground, but she had forgotten her colours. Her friend Ben said they could make a map of sounds instead. They sat with their teacher and listened.', ['Little marks','Tari drew wavy lines for leaves moving. Ben drew dots for shoes on the path. They did not write anyone’s name or record anyone talking.'],['A different visit','Later, the playground sounded quieter. The children made a second map with a new time at the top. The first map had not been wrong. It had shown another moment.'],['Share the idea','Their class tried guessing which marks meant leaves, footsteps and a closing door. Tari added a key beside the map. Now someone else could understand her quiet drawing without needing her to explain every mark.']],
'9-11':['Tari’s art project began with a missing pencil case. Without coloured pencils, she thought her playground map would be dull. Ben suggested mapping sounds rather than surfaces. Their teacher helped them choose a safe listening spot and agree on what they would leave out: names, private conversations and recordings of other children.', ['Invent a key','They made symbols for rustling leaves, footsteps, a bouncing ball and a door closing. At first, the symbols meant different things to each artist. They compared their pages and agreed on a key. Tari noticed that a map needs a reader as well as a maker.'],['The same place changes','They repeated the activity at another time. The marks changed because the activity around them changed. Ben wanted to combine the pages into one crowded picture. Tari preferred keeping the times visible. That way, a reader could distinguish a busy break from a quiet afternoon rather than imagining every sound happening together.'],['What the map could not say','Their display invited classmates to describe patterns. It did not prove which part of the school was always loudest, or explain how every person experienced the space. Some children noticed sounds the artists had missed. Tari added those comments as questions for another visit. The missing colours had led to a new way of paying attention.']],
'12-14':['Tari’s sound map was meant to be an art exercise, yet it quickly became a lesson in how information is selected. She and Ben listened from a supervised playground spot, drawing symbols for broad sound categories. They deliberately excluded names, private speech and recordings of other children. The question was about a shared environment, not tracking individuals.', ['A legend is an agreement','Their first pages were almost impossible to compare. Tari used dots for footsteps; Ben used dots for a bouncing ball. A common key made the marks interpretable. They also recorded the observation time and location in general terms. The symbols simplified a richer experience, and the group discussed what was lost through that simplification. A tidy map could look complete while reflecting only a few listeners’ choices.'],['Avoid a false combined picture','The second observation took place during a quieter part of the day. Ben proposed layering both maps to create a colourful final piece. Tari worried that this would imply that every sound had occurred together. They kept separate panels with clear times, then added a comparison rather than merging the evidence beyond recognition. Their teacher encouraged them to distinguish an artistic interpretation from a claim about consistent sound levels.'],['Invite another perspective','Classmates read the maps differently. Some noticed details the artists had ignored; others found the space comfortable or distracting for different reasons. The display could not settle everyone’s experience or identify the school’s permanently loudest location. It could begin a discussion about observation, representation and access. Tari included an empty panel inviting readers to suggest another safe observation. The project’s strongest feature turned out to be a visible boundary around what its makers knew, together with room for someone else to notice more.']]},
{'6-8':'How did the key help other people read the map?','9-11':'Why did Tari keep the observation times visible?','12-14':'What would be misleading about combining the two maps without explanation?'},['data','patterns','online-safety'],'library-story'),
story('recipe-for-everyone','A Recipe for Everyone','Test instructions with a new reader',{
'6-8':['Noor wrote instructions for making a pretend fruit plate from paper shapes. “Put it there,” she wrote. Her cousin Ayo looked around. “Put what where?” Noor laughed. The instructions made sense in her own head.', ['Name the things','She changed the line to “Put the red circle in the middle of the plate.” Ayo knew what to do this time.'],['Try it again','Noor watched without pointing or fixing the shapes herself. One more step needed a clearer word. They changed it together.'],['Keep both pages','They kept the muddled first recipe and the clearer one. At the next club meeting, the children could see how the words had improved. Noor discovered that a helpful instruction tells another person enough to begin.']],
'9-11':['Noor’s craft club was creating pretend fruit plates from coloured paper. She wrote a recipe for a cousin who had not seen the finished design. The first line said, “Put it there.” Ayo held three shapes and looked at the empty plate. Noor was surprised. She could see exactly what she meant.', ['Try a reader’s view','Instead of explaining every step aloud, Noor asked Ayo to show where the instructions stopped helping. She realised that “it”, “there” and “the usual way” depended on knowledge her reader did not have. She replaced them with specific objects and positions. She also added a list of materials before the first action.'],['Watch without rescuing','For the next test, Noor kept her hands away from the paper and resisted pointing. Ayo completed most of the plate but reversed two steps. They changed the order of those instructions and tried again. Their teacher explained that the test was about improving the instructions, not deciding whether Ayo was good at crafts.'],['A useful version','The final page was shorter in some places and clearer in others. Noor kept a small drawing where words alone had caused confusion. She labelled each revision so the club could follow the changes. Her recipe no longer required its author to stand beside the reader, silently filling every gap with a finger.']],
'12-14':['Noor thought writing instructions would be the easy part of the craft-club project. She could assemble the paper fruit plate in a minute. Her cousin Ayo, reading the first draft, stopped at “put it there”. The problem was not a difficult technique. It was the invisible knowledge Noor had assumed the reader already shared.', ['Make assumptions visible','They began by identifying the intended reader and the starting conditions. Would the shapes already be cut? Which materials were needed? What counted as the middle of the plate? Noor replaced vague references with concrete descriptions and arranged the steps in the order a new reader would encounter them. A labelled sketch explained one spatial relationship more clearly than a long paragraph.'],['Observe the instructions in use','During the next test, Noor resisted the urge to correct Ayo halfway through each step. Every extra gesture from her would conceal a gap in the written version. She asked him to describe where he was uncertain, without treating the test as an examination of his ability. Two steps remained ambiguous. They revised those parts and preserved the previous version, allowing the club to compare exactly what had changed.'],['Clarity is a relationship','Ayo eventually completed the craft without help. That did not mean the instructions were perfect for every possible reader. Another learner might need a different format, larger text or additional explanation. Noor added a note inviting feedback and offered both the written version and a demonstration. She had learned that concise writing was not simply the smallest word count. Useful instructions gave the intended reader enough information, in an accessible order, to act without guessing what the author had left in her head. The paper fruit plate was attractive. The tested recipe was the more interesting invention.']]},
{'6-8':'Why was “put it there” hard to follow?','9-11':'Why did Noor stop pointing during the test?','12-14':'How might the instructions need to change for another audience?'},['algorithms','data','patterns'],'chapati-story'),
story('borrowed-colour','The Borrowed Colour','Credit another person’s idea',{
'6-8':['Leo wanted to paint a giant fish for the class wall. He loved the blue pattern in his friend Hana’s sketchbook. He began copying it, then stopped. Hana had worked hard on that pattern.', ['Ask about it','Leo asked whether he could use the idea in a new painting. Hana said yes, and told him how she had made the shapes.'],['Make something together','They changed the size, added their own colours and painted different parts of the fish. The new picture did not belong to only one idea.'],['Tell the story','Beside the painting, they wrote both names and thanked the class for suggestions. Leo liked explaining which parts they had invented together. Giving Hana credit did not make his work smaller. It made its story more honest.']],
'9-11':['Leo’s fish mural needed a pattern. Hana’s sketchbook contained one he admired: a chain of blue shapes that seemed to swim across the page. He started copying it onto his design, then paused. He would be upset if someone presented his own drawing as their invention. He decided to ask.', ['A conversation before a brush','Hana was happy to collaborate, but she wanted her original sketch recognised. She explained the choices behind it. Leo realised he had noticed the appearance without understanding the work. Together, they tried a larger version and adjusted it to the fish’s curved outline.'],['More than changing a colour','They made new sketches instead of claiming that a different shade automatically made the pattern entirely Leo’s. Some parts remained close to Hana’s original; others developed through their joint experiments. They kept a record showing the source sketch and the later versions. Their teacher helped them discuss permission and credit in age-appropriate terms.'],['Names beside the picture','The finished mural included both artists’ names and a short description of the collaboration. They also thanked classmates whose suggestions had been used. Leo enjoyed telling visitors how the work had grown. Crediting an influence did not spoil the painting’s originality. It showed that making something new can involve learning from someone while still being honest about their contribution.']],
'12-14':['Leo had the outline of a fish mural and no satisfying pattern to fill it. Hana’s sketchbook offered a tempting solution: a sequence of blue shapes with an almost liquid rhythm. He began transferring the design, then wondered how he would describe the result. Calling it wholly his own would erase the work that had made it possible.', ['Permission is a conversation','He asked Hana whether she wanted to collaborate and explained exactly which part interested him. She agreed, provided the original sketch was credited. They discussed where the mural would appear and how their names would be shown. Their teacher helped them think through the agreement without pretending that one classroom conversation answered every question about creative ownership.'],['Keep a visible trail','They developed several alternatives, testing how the pattern worked around the curved outline. Some features closely followed Hana’s sketch; others emerged through joint experimentation. Leo initially suggested that changing the colour would make the source irrelevant. Looking at the versions side by side showed why that explanation was weak. They kept the sketches together, making the relationship between inspiration, borrowing and new contribution easier to describe.'],['An honest account of making','The final label named both artists, credited the original pattern and acknowledged classmates whose suggestions had shaped the finished piece. It did not inflate every casual comment into authorship, nor hide substantial contributions behind the word “inspiration”. Visitors could ask about the process and see the source with Hana’s permission. Leo discovered that originality was not a performance of having learned from nobody. The more useful question was whether the work added something worthwhile while representing its influences and permissions honestly. The fish looked brighter once he no longer had to dodge questions about where its pattern had begun.']]},
{'6-8':'What did Leo do before using Hana’s pattern?','9-11':'Why was changing the colour not the whole answer?','12-14':'How did keeping the sketches help the artists describe their contributions?'},['patterns','online-safety','data'],'library-story'),
story('cup-of-questions','The Cup of Questions','Ask for clarification kindly',{
'6-8':['Every Friday, Sam put paper questions into a cup for the class to explore. One said, “Why is it like that?” Sam turned the paper over. There was no picture and no other clue.', ['Find the missing piece','The teacher asked the writer what “it” meant. It was a seedling leaning towards a window. Now the class knew what to look at.'],['Ask a clearer question','Together, they wrote, “Why is this seedling leaning towards the window?” They drew its pot and the window beside it.'],['Keep wondering','The question did not need to be perfect at the start. Asking for help had made it clearer. Sam added a new sign beside the cup: “Big questions welcome. We can work on the words together.”']],
'9-11':['Sam’s Friday question cup held folded scraps about planets, puddles and broken pencil sharpeners. One week, he opened a slip that read, “Why is it like that?” He was tempted to throw it away as a bad question. His teacher asked what information would make it easier to understand.', ['A question behind the words','The writer had been looking at a seedling leaning towards a window. “It” meant the plant, and “that” meant its slant. Once those details were spoken, the question became interesting. Sam realised that an unclear sentence could still contain a useful observation. The writer needed clarification, not embarrassment.'],['Separate the parts','The class wrote down what had been noticed, then what they wanted to know. They added a drawing of the plant’s position. Their teacher helped them find an appropriate explanation and discuss what a careful investigation might involve. They did not claim that the question alone proved what had caused the leaning.'],['A kinder cup','Sam changed the activity rules. When a slip was unclear, the class could ask a neutral question such as “Which part do you mean?” Nobody had to reveal personal information to participate. The cup became more useful because children were less worried about getting every word right before their curiosity was welcome.']],
'12-14':['Sam liked running the class question cup because it made curiosity visible. He was less patient with a slip that said only, “Why is it like that?” He called it an empty question. His teacher asked whether it was empty or whether the context had been left inside the writer’s head.', ['Recover the context','The writer described a seedling leaning towards a classroom window. The vague words now had referents. Sam could distinguish an observable feature from the explanation being sought. He rewrote the question with the writer rather than replacing it with a question of his own. Their teacher also checked that no identifying or private details were needed for the discussion.'],['Do not turn a question into evidence','The class separated three things: what had been observed, which explanation seemed possible and what further investigation would help distinguish alternatives. They consulted suitable learning material with their teacher. A clearer question made the investigation more manageable, but it did not establish a cause by itself. Sam noticed how easily a suggested explanation could become a supposedly proven fact when repeated several times around the room.'],['Design a better conversation','He revised the cup’s instructions to welcome unfinished questions. Readers could ask for clarification without mocking the writer or demanding unnecessary personal context. A useful follow-up might identify the object, the comparison or the time period. The aim was shared understanding, not a contest over who could phrase a perfect sentence first. At the next session, a younger pupil asked a question that initially sounded equally vague. Sam reached for a pencil instead of the wastebasket. The cup had become more intelligent because its host had learned to interpret uncertainty with patience and to ask what was missing before deciding there was nothing worth answering.']]},
{'6-8':'What did “it” mean in the question?','9-11':'How could you ask for a missing detail kindly?','12-14':'Why does improving a question not prove its suggested explanation?'},['sensors','data','water-cycle'],'rain-story'),
story('last-piece-puzzle','The Last Piece Was a Question','Check the rule before continuing',{
'6-8':['Ada made a row of coloured paper shapes: circle, square, circle, square. Her friend Mo added a circle. Then Ada showed another row. It began the same way but ended differently.', ['Say your rule','Mo had guessed the next shape without hearing the rule. Ada asked him to explain his idea. He said, “I was taking turns between two shapes.”'],['More than one idea','Ada could think of another rule for a short row. They tried making the rows longer so their ideas were easier to compare.'],['A puzzle for a friend','They wrote their rules on folded cards and let a friend ask questions before choosing a shape. The best answer was not always the fastest one. Sometimes it began with, “Can you show me a little more?”']],
'9-11':['Ada’s puzzle looked easy: circle, square, circle, square. Mo supplied another circle and declared victory. Ada asked him which rule he was following. “Take turns,” he replied. She showed a second card whose short beginning looked the same but belonged to a different, longer arrangement.', ['Explain the guess','The class discussed why Mo’s answer was reasonable under his proposed rule. It was not the only possible continuation of a short sequence, though classroom puzzles often have a simple intended pattern. Ada wanted readers to say the rule aloud instead of treating one shape as an answer that explained itself.'],['Ask for another clue','Mo requested more terms. Ada revealed enough of her longer pattern for the difference to become visible. They tested each proposed rule against every term shown, rather than choosing a favourite and ignoring the awkward parts. One suggested rule fitted the first card but failed on the extra clue.'],['A fairer puzzle','They rewrote the instructions to explain what sort of pattern to look for. The finished game rewarded both a continuation and a description of the rule. Ada kept a separate challenge inviting several possible answers with explanations. Mo discovered that asking for clarification could be a clever move rather than an admission that he had stopped thinking.']],
'12-14':['Mo saw Ada’s alternating shapes and supplied the next circle almost immediately. She asked him to state the generating rule. He had assumed an indefinitely repeating two-shape unit. The visible terms supported that interpretation, but they did not uniquely determine every possible continuation. Ada’s actual card contained a longer repeated block with the same opening.', ['A plausible rule is an assumption','The discussion did not make Mo’s reasoning worthless. Under a common simple-rule assumption, his prediction was sensible. The problem was presenting that assumption as if it had been established by the short sequence alone. Ada asked classmates to distinguish the observed terms, the proposed rule and the prediction that followed from it. Those were related pieces of reasoning, not interchangeable statements.'],['Use new information','Mo requested further terms. The additional clue separated his initial rule from Ada’s intended one. Other classmates proposed alternatives and tested them against the complete set of visible terms. A rule that explained only the convenient beginning was not enough. They also considered the difference between a puzzle designed around one intended answer and an open question allowing several justified models.'],['Write the task honestly','Ada revised the classroom version to state the relevant constraints and ask for an explanation alongside the next item. For a creative version, she explicitly invited multiple continuations, provided the proposed rule accounted for everything shown. Mo preferred the second activity because it exposed the reasoning rather than only marking a shape right or wrong. The last piece of the puzzle was a question about what the instructions meant. Once that piece was included, the task became both fairer and more interesting, with less room for confidence to stand in for an argument.']]},
{'6-8':'What rule was Mo using?','9-11':'Why was asking for more terms useful?','12-14':'How do the task’s constraints affect whether a continuation is uniquely justified?'},['patterns','fractions','data'],'chapati-story'),
story('repair-cafe','The Saturday Repair Table','Know when to ask for help',{
'6-8':['At the community hall, adults set up a repair table. Biko brought a torn storybook. His friend brought a toy with a loose wheel. Biko wanted to fix everything before lunch.', ['Different jobs','The grown-ups gave the children safe paper jobs. They showed Biko how to support a torn page. The toy went to an adult who knew how to check it.'],['A useful question','Someone brought a broken electrical object. Biko did not plug it in or open it. He asked the repair organiser for help. Knowing when to stop was part of being helpful.'],['More than new things','By the end, Biko had mended one book and made labels for several others. He had not repaired everything. He had helped in a careful way, and the storybook was ready for another reader.']],
'9-11':['Biko arrived at the community repair table with a torn storybook and an ambitious plan to fix everything in the hall. The adult organisers had a different plan: match tasks with skills, supervise children and keep unsafe equipment out of reach. Biko was assigned books, paper and labels. At first, that sounded unimportant.', ['A small repair matters','A volunteer showed him how to assess the torn page and use suitable materials. Biko worked slowly and asked before trying an unfamiliar method. He discovered that a rushed repair could hide words or make the page difficult to turn. The next reader mattered more than making the damage disappear from a distance.'],['A boundary is useful','When a broken electrical item arrived, Biko wanted to see inside. The organiser explained that it belonged with an appropriately skilled adult. Children were not to open, connect or test it. Biko carried a note to the repair desk instead. He could contribute without taking over a task he was not equipped to do safely.'],['A record of care','The team labelled each item with its status: ready, waiting or needing specialist help. Nobody called an unfinished object safe merely because it looked tidy. Biko’s repaired book returned to the reading shelf. His biggest discovery was that being useful included recognising the limits of his knowledge and finding the right person to ask.']],
'12-14':['Biko expected the community repair event to reward whoever fixed the most objects. The organisers began with a briefing about supervision, competence and tasks that would be left to specialists. He was disappointed to receive a stack of damaged books. He had imagined doing something more impressive than paper repair.', ['Define a successful repair','A volunteer helped him assess a torn page, choose suitable materials and consider how the book would be used. A repair that covered words, trapped a page or quickly failed was not successful merely because it looked neat. Biko began checking usability rather than only appearance. He also learned to stop and ask before trying a technique he had not been shown.'],['Do not confuse confidence with competence','A broken electrical item attracted his curiosity. The organisers kept it disconnected and assigned it to an appropriately qualified adult. Biko was not asked to open or test it. His useful role was to record its status and direct the owner to the repair lead. The boundary did not depend on whether he felt brave or whether an online video made the task look simple. Some tasks required training, appropriate equipment and responsibilities he did not hold.'],['Leave an honest status','The team used clear labels for completed work, items awaiting attention and objects needing specialist assessment. They did not equate a tidy exterior with a verified safe condition. Biko helped write a short description of what had actually been done to each book, including work left unfinished. By the afternoon, he no longer measured the event by the number of objects he personally controlled. He had contributed several careful repairs, made the handover information clearer and protected the next user from misleading claims. Asking the right person had become a practical skill, rather than something to apologise for.']]},
{'6-8':'What did Biko do when a job needed a skilled adult?','9-11':'Why was “waiting for help” a useful label?','12-14':'How did Biko’s idea of a successful repair change?'},['online-safety','algorithms','data'],'library-story'),
story('two-routes-home','Two Routes to the Same Place','Compare the whole journey',{
'6-8':['Yara and her aunt drew two pretend routes for a toy bus. One line on the paper was short. The other was long. “The short one must be quickest,” Yara said. Then her aunt added three toy stops.', ['Count the waiting','The bus waited at every stop. Yara counted slowly while her aunt moved it. The shorter line now took longer in their game.'],['Tell the rules','They wrote down the rules: how far the bus moved each turn and how long it waited. They were testing a model on paper, not choosing a real road alone.'],['Look at more than length','Yara made another map with a bridge and a busy crossing. She asked what else might change a journey. A line could show distance, but it could not tell the whole story by itself.']],
'9-11':['Yara’s aunt gave her a paper map and a toy bus. Two routes linked the same model houses. Yara chose the shorter line immediately. Then her aunt introduced a rule: the bus must wait two turns at each marked stop. The short route had several stops. The long one had none.', ['Keep track of the whole trip','They counted movement turns and waiting turns separately. Under the game’s rules, the shorter path took longer. Yara had compared only one part of the journey. She wrote a table showing distance in model squares, number of stops and total turns so another player could check her calculation.'],['A model has limits','They changed the waiting rule and tried again. The result changed too. Yara realised that the answer depended on assumptions, not on a permanent truth that longer routes were faster. Their paper bus did not include real traffic, road conditions or people’s safety needs. Real travel decisions stayed with a trusted adult.'],['Ask a better question','For the class puzzle, Yara replaced “Which route is best?” with a more precise question about total turns under the stated rules. She added a discussion about other things a real traveller might care about. Her aunt liked the new question. It gave the children something they could calculate and something important that a calculation alone could not decide.']],
'12-14':['Yara expected the shortest drawn route to win the toy-bus challenge. Her aunt added waiting times at stops, and the result reversed under their chosen rules. The exercise exposed a familiar shortcut in reasoning: treating a convenient measure as if it represented the entire objective.', ['Choose the quantity you mean','They recorded movement and waiting turns separately, then calculated the model journey’s total duration. Yara labelled the units clearly. A route’s length and its travel time were related within the model but were not identical quantities. Another player could inspect the assumptions and reproduce the arithmetic. When they changed the delay at a stop, they recalculated rather than defending the original winner.'],['Do not overextend the model','The paper activity deliberately omitted many real-world conditions. It could not recommend a safe journey, predict actual traffic or replace an adult’s travel judgement. Yara listed the omissions beside the map. She also noted that “best” might refer to several objectives: time, accessibility, comfort or another need. Assigning a single score without explaining the chosen priorities would hide a value judgement inside a number.'],['Make the question answerable','For the classroom version, she asked which route required the fewest turns under specified movement and delay rules. That question had a checkable answer. A second prompt invited learners to discuss which additional considerations would matter outside the game. Yara’s aunt suggested adding a route that was slightly slower but worked for a wider toy vehicle. The activity grew from a race into a design discussion. Yara kept the shortest line on the page, but it no longer carried the authority to settle every question about a journey simply because it was easiest to measure.']]},
{'6-8':'What did the short route have that made the toy bus wait?','9-11':'Why did changing the rules change the result?','12-14':'Which values can be hidden inside the word “best”?'},['algorithms','data','fractions'],'robot-story')
];
